.TH REMOVE_ORPHANS 1 "2026-08-26" "rptsched-cleanup" "rptsched-cleanup Manual"
.SH NAME
remove_orphans.py \- quarantine orphaned files in a Symphony Rptsched directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B remove_orphans.py
.B \-\-data\-dir
.I DIR
.B \-\-quarantine\-dir
.I DIR
[\fB\-\-execute\fR | \fB\-\-restore\fR \fIRUN_DIR\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B remove_orphans.py
finds
.I orphan
file groups in a Symphony ILS report\-scheduler data directory
(\fIrptsched\fR) and moves them into a quarantine directory.

An orphan is any group of files sharing a 4\-character alphanumeric id
prefix (\fI<id>.*\fR, e.g. \fIabcd.set\fR, \fIabcd.selans\fR) for which the
.I schedlist
index file in \fB\-\-data\-dir\fR contains no matching line. Every
legitimate saved template has exactly one \fIschedlist\fR line for its id;
files whose id is absent from \fIschedlist\fR are considered abandoned and
are candidates for removal.

This tool never deletes anything: matching files are moved (not copied)
into a timestamped run subfolder under \fB\-\-quarantine\-dir\fR, and a
\fImanifest.csv\fR is written recording the original location of every
moved file, so the run can later be reversed with \fB\-\-restore\fR.

Unlike the stale\-templates and scheduled\-report categories, orphans have
no corresponding \fIschedlist\fR line to begin with, so this tool never
modifies \fIschedlist\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-data\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Path to the \fIrptsched\fR data directory to scan. Required in every mode,
including \fB\-\-restore\fR. Never hardcoded, so the same script can be
pointed at a local test copy before ever touching the production
directory.
.TP
.BR \-\-quarantine\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Path to the quarantine directory that holds run subfolders. Created if it
does not already exist. Required in every mode.
.TP
.B \-\-execute
Perform the move. Without this flag the script runs in dry\-run mode:
it reports what would be moved and writes nothing.
.TP
.BR \-\-restore " " \fIRUN_DIR\fR
Reverse a previous \fB\-\-execute\fR run by moving every file recorded in
\fIRUN_DIR\fR/manifest.csv back to its original location. Mutually
exclusive with \fB\-\-execute\fR.
.SH MODES
.SS Dry run (default)
With neither \fB\-\-execute\fR nor \fB\-\-restore\fR given, the script only
prints what it found: the count of orphan ids and files, followed by one
line per orphan id listing its files. Nothing on disk is touched.
.SS Execute
With \fB\-\-execute\fR, the script:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
Creates \fB\-\-quarantine\-dir\fR/orphans_\fITIMESTAMP\fR (\fITIMESTAMP\fR
is \fIYYYYMMDD_HHMMSS\fR at the moment the run starts).
.IP 2. 4
Moves every file in every orphan group into that run subfolder.
.IP 3. 4
Writes \fImanifest.csv\fR into the run subfolder with one row per moved
file (id, filename, extension, source_path, dest_path, moved_at).
.RE
.IP
If a move fails partway through, the files already moved stay moved, a
partial \fImanifest.csv\fR is written for exactly those files, and the
script exits non\-zero. Nothing is rolled back automatically \- resolve
the underlying error (e.g. a permissions problem) and either re\-run
against the remaining orphans or use \fB\-\-restore\fR on the partial run.
.SS Restore
With \fB\-\-restore\fR \fIRUN_DIR\fR, the script reads
\fIRUN_DIR\fR/manifest.csv and moves each file from its recorded
\fIdest_path\fR back to its recorded \fIsource_path\fR.

Restore is idempotent and resumable: any manifest row whose
\fIdest_path\fR no longer exists is treated as already restored and
silently skipped. If a row's \fIsource_path\fR already exists (e.g. a
same\-id file was recreated since quarantine), the script aborts
immediately with a non\-zero exit rather than overwriting it. Re\-running
\fB\-\-restore\fR on the same \fIRUN_DIR\fR is always safe \- it simply
skips whatever was already restored and resumes from where it left off.
\fImanifest.csv\fR itself is never deleted, so it doubles as a permanent
audit record of the run.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including a clean dry run, or a restore that skipped
already\-restored rows).
.TP
.B 1
A move or restore operation failed. Details are printed to stderr.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <data\-dir>/schedlist
Read to determine which ids are \fInot\fR orphaned. Never modified by
this tool.
.TP
.I <quarantine\-dir>/orphans_<timestamp>/manifest.csv
Written by \fB\-\-execute\fR; read by \fB\-\-restore\fR. Columns: id,
filename, extension, source_path, dest_path, moved_at.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Preview what would be removed, against a local test copy:
.B remove_orphans.py \-\-data\-dir ./rptsched \-\-quarantine\-dir ./quarantine
.TP
Actually quarantine the orphans found above:
.B remove_orphans.py \-\-data\-dir ./rptsched \-\-quarantine\-dir ./quarantine \-\-execute
.TP
Undo a specific run:
.B remove_orphans.py \-\-data\-dir ./rptsched \-\-quarantine\-dir ./quarantine \-\-restore ./quarantine/orphans_20260826_140501
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR remove_stale_templates.py (1),
.BR sync_operator_field.py (1)

Domain reference: \fIrptsched\-domain\-reference.md\fR in the
rptsched\-cleanup repository, in particular the definition of
.I orphan
under "Definitions".
.SH CAVEATS
Requires Python 3.6.8, standard library only \- there is no dependable way
to install third\-party packages on the production RHEL8 host this tool
targets.

Quarantine is always a move, never a delete. Nothing this tool does is
destructive on its own; data loss would require also deleting the
quarantine directory afterward.
